 question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: In a world where computers are increasingly powerful and are concealed within ever more glossy slabs of aluminum, the Raspberry Pi (RPi) offers surprising proof for the virtue of moderation. Resembling little more than a credit card-sized scrap of exposed circuit board, the RPi is a fully programmable PC that runs a free, open-source Linux operating system, plugs into any TV, can power 3D graphics, connects to the Internet and, with a little ingenuity, be used to create your own personalized robot slave. The computer's miniature frame is crowded with two USB ports, an SD card slot, an Ethernet connection and microchip in the middle -- all powered by a universal USB mobile charger.\n@highlight\nThe Raspberry Pi is a credit-card sized computer that costs little more than a textbook\n@highlight\nOriginally designed to encourage school children to learn programming skills\n@highlight\nThe mini-PC now hugely popular with DIY geeks, who have used it to power their own creations\nQuery: It turned out there was a voracious appetite -- particularly among a growing class of @placeholder geeks -- for a cheap, easily-programmable, open-source piece of hardware that would allow them to let their imaginations run wild.\n\nOptions : A. Raspberry Pi\nB. Ethernet\nC. Internet\nD. USB\nE. Linux\nF. RPi\nG. DIY\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 386, "output_tokens": 2, "total_tokens": 388}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_11586", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: (CNN) For several weeks now, city and state officials have traded barbs over who is ultimately responsible for the toxic water that's inundated Flint, Michigan. On Thursday, the feds said they both are. In an emergency administrative order, the Environmental Protection Agency slammed the city of Flint, the Michigan Department of Environmental Quality and the State of Michigan, saying they are all responsible for the lead contamination in the city's water. Their responses to the crisis, the EPA said, were \"inadequate to protect public health.\" EPA administrator resigns \"There are serious, ongoing concerns with delays, lack of adequate transparency, and capacity to safely manage the drinking water system,\" the agency said in a press release.\n@highlight\nThe EPA says the city of Flint and the state of Michigan are among those responsible for the water crisis\n@highlight\nThe agency's regional administrator who oversees Flint has resigned\n@highlight\nCongress may hold a hearing on the incident\nQuery: The emergency order also means the EPA will start doing its own lead testing in @placeholder's water system.\n\nOptions : A. Michigan\nB. EPA\nC. Michigan Department of Environmental Quality\nD. State of Michigan\nE. Flint\nF. Congress\nG. Environmental Protection Agency\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 317, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 323}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_16180", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: Since the mid-1990s, Rs 1,500 crore has been spent to clean up the dying Yamuna, but it all has gone down the drain. Now the water you drink is expected to give a new lease of life to the once fabled river. For the first time in history, Delhi may get its share of Yamuna water, treated and supplied to households, from the river itself. The Capital has so far been getting water diverted from the river to Western Yamuna Canal (WYC) in Haryana. This meets Delhi\u2019s 70 per cent drinking water needs. A high-powered committee headed by the secretary, ministry of water resources, Shashi Shekhar, has recommended this move to the National Green Tribunal (NGT) to ensure fresh water flow in the river.\n@highlight\nCommittee says Yamuna river will remain a 'sewer' unless action is taken\n@highlight\nIt will take time for the river to revive, but Green Tribunal supports plan\nQuery: Even now the canal water is deposited at Palla in @placeholder and collected at Wazirabad before it is cleaned up and supplied.\n\nOptions : A. Western Yamuna Canal\nB. WYC\nC. Haryana\nD. Shashi Shekhar\nE. National Green Tribunal\nF. Green Tribunal\nG. The Capital\nH. Delhi\nI. Committee\nJ. Rs\nK. Yamuna\nL. NGT\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 354, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 360}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_16687", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: Users\u00a0of Microsoft's new Windows 10 software were elated to find the firm had brought back solitaire, the famous free game. In its latest incarnation it boasts more features and can even track your stats. However, there is just one problem - to play without ads, users have to pay Microsoft $10 a year, every year. Experts have describe the decision as odd. 'Netflix charges you $7.99 per month for thousands of streaming movies and TV shows, Spotify, Slacker, Rdio and others charge about $10 per month for unlimited music,' said Mark Hachman of PC World. 'And Microsoft? They want you to pay $1.50 per month to remove ads from Solitaire.'\n@highlight\nFree game was a popular part of earlier windows versions\n@highlight\nNew Version charges users $10 a year for ad free\u00a0Solitaire\u00a0games\n@highlight\nExperts say the feature is 'an accident waiting to happen'\nQuery: '@placeholder itself is free, so complaining too much about this might seem ungracious,' said Hachman.\n\nOptions : A. Windows 10\nB. Solitaire\nC. Rdio\nD. Netflix\nE. Microsoft\nF. PC World\nG. Slacker\nH. New Version\nI. Mark Hachman\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 333, "output_tokens": 2, "total_tokens": 335}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_18477", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: (CNN) People are often surprised to learn that Shelby Payne has type 1 diabetes -- because her identical twin sister, Sydney, does not. \"Everyone's like, 'Wow, how in the world does (my sister) not have it?' \" said Payne, 23, who once played forward on Stanford's soccer team, like her sister. \"They can't really fathom it.\" Though experts agree that genetics influence who gets type 1 diabetes, Payne's story suggests that something other than genes must also be at play. And some researchers are focusing in on one family of viruses in particular. A new study from Finland joins decades of research suggesting that enteroviruses, which include over 100 individual virus types, may play a key role in triggering type 1 diabetes, which has no cure.\n@highlight\nType 1 diabetes is an autoimmune condition, which may be triggered by factors that are not genetic\n@highlight\nEnteroviruses, known to affect the gut, may be a key player in the development of type 1 diabetes\nQuery: @placeholder and her sister have also enrolled in TrialNet, a diabetes research network studying ways to prevent and delay type 1 diabetes.\n\nOptions : A. Enteroviruses\nB. Type 1\nC. Payne\nD. Finland\nE. Sydney\nF. Stanford\nG. Shelby Payne\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{G}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 364, "output_tokens": 2, "total_tokens": 366}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_9449", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: She won an Oscar for playing Elizabeth II in the 2006 film The Queen. Now, Helen Mirren has once again scored a major award for playing her Royal Highness - this time for the play The Audience - at the 2015 Tony Awards in New York City on Sunday night. It also marked the 69-year-old's first ever Tony Award win, which is considered to be U.S. theater's highest honor. The Tony Award meant that Mirren was only a Grammy Award away from gaining so-called EGOT status as the winner of an Emmy, Grammy, Oscar and Tony - a distinction shared by only 12 entertainers.\n@highlight\nHelen Mirren wins for Best Leading Actress in a Play for portrayal of Queen Elizabeth II in The Audience\n@highlight\nThe Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-Time scores five awards including Best Play and Best Leading Actor in a Play\n@highlight\nFun Home ties for lead with five awards including \u00a0Best Musical, Best Book of a Musical, 