f tomb\n@highlight\nDecorative elements resemble those where his body is\n@highlight\nSuleiman's death at Szigetvar was kept secret for 48 days\n@highlight\nExcavation work at the site would restart in April\nQuery: Until his death at age 71, Suleiman was the @placeholder's longest-ruling sultan.\n\nOptions : A. Suleiman\nB. Norbet Pap\nC. Szigetvar\nD. Regional and Development Studies\nE. University of Pecs\nF. Istanbul\nG. Political Geography\nH. Hungary\nI. Magnificent\nJ. Ottoman Empire\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{J}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 340, "output_tokens": 5, "total_tokens": 345}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_17691", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: Nasa wants to land the first humans on Mars in 2035, and to do that, it needs the world's toughest parachute. Its latest plan is to use a large inflatable 'flying saucer' that will slow a spacecraft down after piercing through the thin Martian atmosphere. Dubbed the density supersonic decelerator (LDSD), the space agency plans to conduct an ambitious test flight of its design on Wednesday at 1:30pm (5:30pm GMT). The LDSD is unique in its design, using a donut-shaped inflatable decelerator to create atmospheric drag. When attached to the exterior of a capsule travelling at Mach 4 it will be capable of slowing it down to Mach 2, where a regular parachute can then deploy.\n@highlight\nThe saucer is known as the low density supersonic decelerator\n@highlight\nThe test flight will take place on Wednesday at 1:30pm ET (5:30 GMT)\n@highlight\nInflatable saucer is 5ft (4.6 metres) wide, weighing 7,000lbs (3.200kg)\n@highlight\nThe parachute was torn apart in when it deployed in a flight last year\nQuery: Last June, to simulate the @placeholder atmosphere on Earth, the LDSD was lifted to a height of 23 miles (37km) by a high-altitude balloon.\n\nOptions : A. Martian\nB. Nasa\nC. LDSD\nD. Inflatable\nE. GMT\nF. Mars\nG. Mach 2\nH. Mach 4\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The passage discusses the LDSD being tested for use on Mars, and the query says the test was done to simulate the Martian atmosphere on Earth.  \n\nThe correct option is **A. Martian**.  \n\n\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 369, "output_tokens": 49, "total_tokens": 418}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_14310", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: (CNN) -- After downloading the latest iPhone, iPod touch and iPad operating system, iOS 6, the fun part is excitedly poking around and hunting for what's new. Find subtle design changes, like the phone's key pad, or set up new features, like Facebook integration. You might even use Siri again for the first time in a while to test its improved sports and restaurant knowledge. If you have an iPhone 3GS, 4 or 4S, a fourth generation iPod touch, or an iPad 2 or the new iPad, you can upgrade to iOS 6. Go to your settings screen, tap General and then Software Update. Keep in mind that some features are limited to the most recent models.\n@highlight\nNew operating system update for the iPhone, iPad and iPod touch has gained and lost features\n@highlight\nApple's new Maps app drops Google data, including public transit directions, adds turn-by-turn\n@highlight\nVoice assistant Siri adds movie, sports and restaurant data\nQuery: Flyover is only available on the iPhone 4S and later, @placeholder 2 and later, and the iPod touch.\n\nOptions : A. iPhone 3GS\nB. General\nC. Software Update\nD. Google\nE. iPhone\nF. Facebook\nG. iOS\nH. iPod touch\nI. iPad\nJ. Apple\nK. Siri\nL. Maps\nM. CNN\nN. 4S\nO. iOS 6\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{I}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 359, "output_tokens": 2, "total_tokens": 361}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_12184", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: The identity of the kissing couple immortalized in the throes of passions in Times Square on VJ Day, 1945 has been thrown into doubt after a new scientific theory disproved all existing accounts. More than a dozen people claimed to be either the the sailor or the nurse shown passionately embracing as the news broke of the end of the Second World War after Japan's surrender. But according to a team of astronomers, none of their accounts can be true. The scientists say they have managed to pinpoint the time of the shot by analyzing the shadows in the image - and their result does not match any of the stories put forward.\n@highlight\nSailor and a nurse were immortalized in photograph from August 14, 1945\n@highlight\nWas published in Life magazine - but photographer didn't get their names\n@highlight\nMore than a dozen people have claimed they were in the image\n@highlight\nScientists have now debunked some stories via astronomy\n@highlight\nSay they have pinned time of image to 5:51pm - which does not match any leading stories\nQuery: He remembers tearing through @placeholder kissing everyone in sight from sheer excitement.\n\nOptions : A. Second World War\nB. Japan\nC. Life\nD. Sailor\nE. Times Square\nF. VJ Day\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{F}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 365, "output_tokens": 2, "total_tokens": 367}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_11394", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: Good news has arrived for stranded holidaymakers after Virgin Australia and Jetstar officially announced that conditions were safe to resume flights to and from Bali. Earlier on Saturday, Indonesian officials declared Denpasar Airport safe to reopen as the cloud of ash from the eruption of Mount Raung drifted away. Virgin sent an observation flight to the airport to have visual confirmation and determine whether conditions were suitable for flights to operate before giving the all-clear. The airline said scheduled flights out of Denpasar would go ahead on Saturday evening and it plans to continue operations in and out of the Indonesian island on Sunday.\n@highlight\nVirgin Australia and Jetstar has confirmed it is able to resume flights\n@highlight\nThey announced the conditions were safe to operate to and from Bali\n@highlight\nStrong winds  pushed the ash cloud away from the airport on Saturday\n@highlight\nAn eruption from\u00a0Mount Raung caused the large ash cloud to form\n@highlight\nThe cloud caused visibility issue and can effect engine electronics\n@highlight\nIt caused thousands of Australians to be stranded at Denpasar airport\nQuery: The ash grounded planes from Virgin, @placeholder, Garuda and AirAsia on Friday.\n\nOptions : A. Mount Raung\nB. Denpasar\nC. Virgin Australia\nD. Bali\nE. Virgin\nF. Australians\nG. Indonesian\nH. Jetstar\n