en_usage": {"input_tokens": 336, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 342}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_12028", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: Banish the tumour and cure the disease. That\u2019s what a Delhi-based think-tank close to the RSS is set to do by posing a serious legal challenge to Kashmir\u2019s \u2018special\u2019 provisions. The Delhi-based Jammu and Kashmir Study Centre (JKSC) is gearing up to target Article 35A, an outgrowth powered by Article 370 of the Constitution, which gives J&K special status. Article 35A allows the state to grant special privileges and rights to its permanent residents, and not to others living there, the think-tank says. To scrap these special provisions, the Jammu and Kashmir Study Centre (JKSC) has decided to approach the Supreme Court. On July 11, the organisation will formally announce at a seminar in Delhi the date for filing the petition.\n@highlight\nThe Jammu and Kashmir Study Centre, based in Delhi, is challenging Article 35A\n@highlight\nThe amendment is powered by Article 370 of the Constitution, which gives J&K special status\n@highlight\nIt lets the state grant privileges and rights to permanent residents, but not others\nQuery: RSS national executive member Arun Kumar is one of the advisers with the petitioner @placeholder.\n\nOptions : A. Jammu and Kashmir Study Centre\nB. JKSC\nC. Article 370\nD. RSS\nE. J&K\nF. Supreme Court\nG. Delhi\nH. Constitution\nI. Article 35A\nJ. Kashmir\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{A}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 362, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 368}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_10093", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: Despite complaints that Google Glass was too bulky and nerdy, rumours suggest the tech giant will release a new version with a larger prism display. The Enterprise Edition (EE) is also said to include an Intel Atom processor for improved performance and battery life. Some users of Glass, which was pulled in January following poor sales and mixed reviews, complained of eye strain after prolonged use. This may explain why Google might have decided to include a larger screen in its next design, 9to5Google reported. It\u2019s rumoured that final iterations of the EE include a prism that extends further, allowing wearers to glance upwards and focus on the screen more comfortably than before.\n@highlight\nRumours suggest Google will release a new version of Google Glass, dubbed the Enterprise Edition, with a larger prism display\n@highlight\nImproved screen could help to reduce eye strain for wearers\n@highlight\nSmart specs are also said to include an Intel Atom processor for improved performance and battery life compared to the previous version\n@highlight\nSome users of Glass, which was pulled in January following poor sales and mixed reviews, complained of eye strain after prolonged use\nQuery: The previous @placeholder headset was launched in a beta version under the firm's Explorer programme.\n\nOptions : A. Google\nB. Google Glass\nC. Intel Atom\nD. Smart\nE. Glass\nF. EE\nG. Enterprise Edition\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "The correct option is B. Google Glass.\n\n\\boxed{B}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 342, "output_tokens": 15, "total_tokens": 357}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_10484", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: In the ancient Inca kingdom, the wheel was yet to be invented, iron was foreign and steel was unheard of. So when the Incas needed to travel across the Andes, they did so on handwoven rope bridges, stretched over gorges and rivers. Five hundred years later, just one of the Inca rope bridges remains: the Keshwa Chaca, which\u00a0spans the Apurimac River near Huinchiri, Peru, in the Province of Canas. Dangerous work: Local villagers work on renewing the Keshwa Chaca - the last Inca rope bridge - in the Andes near Huinchiri, Peru Incredible skill: The 90ft long Keshwa Chaca remains thanks to local villagers who rebuild the bridge each year using the same techniques as their Inca ancestors\n@highlight\nInca kingdom in the Andes was connected via handwoven rope bridges\n@highlight\nToday, only one bridge remains - the Keshwa Chaca near Huinchiri, Peru\n@highlight\nThe 90ft-long bridge is rebuilt by villagers over three days every June, before gradually\u00a0disintegrating\u00a0each time\n@highlight\nLocals use the same techniques to make the rope as their Inca ancestors\nQuery: The last @placeholder disappeared in the early 17th century, and over the years their bridges followed.\n\nOptions : A. Andes\nB. Inca\nC. Peru\nD. Incas\nE. Apurimac River\nF. Keshwa Chaca\nG. Huinchiri\nH. Canas\nI. Province\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 384, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 390}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_18766", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: Everyone has that friend who instigates a wild night out, encourages you to have one drink too many, but gets you home in one piece. Now there's a 'smart beer mug' that does a similar thing. Fitted with sensors, the ePint lights up when it is empty, keeps track of the number of drinks its user has sunk and can even suggest ordering a cab at the end of the night. Start-up Xtreme Tech, based in Los Angeles, California, is raising money to put the gadget into production on Kickstarter. The firm audaciously describes its invention as 'Vegas in a cup'.\n@highlight\nePint contains sensors, LEDs and a Bluetooth chip to link to a phone\n@highlight\nIt lights up to show when it is empty or when a sports team has scored\n@highlight\nApp features let users play drinking games and even order a cab home\n@highlight\nGadget is available to pre-order via Kickstarter from $30 (\u00a320)\nQuery: The ePint also has a built-in weight sensor to track how much liquid it is holding, accelerometers and @placeholder that flash and change colour, as well as a bottle opener so it can be filled with beer quickly.\n\nOptions : A. App\nB. Kickstarter\nC. Bluetooth\nD. California\nE. LEDs\nF. Gadget\nG. Los Angeles\nH. Vegas\nI. Xtreme Tech\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 356, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 362}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_15366", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: William Tallon was more than just the Queen Mother\u2019s favourite servant. He rose from impoverished beginnings to become her confidant and close friend. As the first part of a remarkable new biography revealed last week, Tallon was also a predatory homosexual who breached Royal security by bringing young men back to Clarence House at night. And he made powerful enemies. Here, in the final extract of Backstairs Billy, we reveal that courtiers took their revenge by banishing him from the Queen Mother\u2019s side, shamefully disregarding her wish that he should be allowed to live at Clarence House for the rest of his life.\n@highlight\nQueen Mother's royal courtier William Tallon was known as Backstairs Billy\n@highlight\nRose from impoverished beginnings to become her confidant and friend\n@highlight\nBut he also breached protocol and brought young men to Clarence House\n@highlight\nOther courtiers took revenge by banishing him from Queen Mother's side\n@highlight\nAlso denied her request that he should be allowed to live at Clarence House for the rest of his life\nQuery: For years he had organised lunches and dinners for the @placeholder, but he had never had to make his own meals.\n\nOptions : A. Royal\nB. Mother\nC. Queen\nD. Queen Mother\nE. Clarence House\nF. Tallon\nG. William Tallon\nH. Backstairs Billy\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 348, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 354}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_12613", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: It's the social and health crisis plaguing almost one million Japanese people. The sufferers of 'Hikikomori' \u2013 mostly young men - have such severe social withdrawal they isolate themselves in their bedroom, in some cases for years. One of the few hikikomori experts in Japan, Dr Takahiro Kato, suffered from the condition as a student and is now working to prevent it from having a widespread affect on the next generation. Dr Kato, who is studying hikikomori with a team of experts at Kyushu University in Fukuoka, said he had seen cases so severe that some men in their 50s had been withdrawn from society for more than 30 years.\n@highlight\n'Hikikomori' is a social and health crisis affect almost one million Japanese\n@highlight\nSufferers have such severe social withdrawal they isolate themselves in their bedroom, in some cases for years\n@highlight\nDoctors are working to stop it having a major affect on the next generation\nQuery: 'It\u2019s one reason why @placeholder have many more hikikomori compared to western society.'\n\nOptions : A. Hikikomori\nB. Sufferers\nC. Japan\nD. Kato\nE. Kyushu University\nF. Fukuoka\nG. Japanese\nH. Takahiro Kato\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 345, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 351}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_15745", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: (CNN) You will be able to test yourself at home for a genetic predisposition for certain diseases, thanks to a new ruling from the US Food and Drug Administration. On Thursday, the FDA gave 23andMe permission to market its Personal Genome Service genetic health risk tests for 10 diseases. It is the first direct-to-consumer genetic test the FDA has allowed to provide that information. The tests can look for a genetic predisposition to conditions; the 10 approved Thursday are Parkinson's; late-onset Alzheimer's; celiac disease; a movement disorder called early-onset primary dystonia; a disorder that elevates your risk for lung and liver disease called Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency; a blood clotting disorder Factor XI deficiency; an organ and tissue disorder called Gaucher disease type 1; a red blood cell condition known as G6PD; hereditary hemochromatosis, an iron overload disorder; and hereditary thrombophilia, a blood clot disorder.\n@highlight\n23andMe gets FDA approval to market its genetic tests for 10 diseases\n@highlight\nThese tests do not give a diagnosis but suggest whether you have a predisposition\nQuery: Environmental factors and lifestyle choices probably influence whether someone will get a disease like late-onset @placeholder's.\n\nOptions : A. US Food and Drug Administration\nB. Alpha-1\nC. Personal Genome Service\nD. G6PD\nE. Alzheimer\nF. Gaucher\nG. FDA\nH. Parkinson\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the contetn of the correct option. ", "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2", "generated_result": {"generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}", "success": true, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 353, "output_tokens": 6, "total_tokens": 359}}, "cost": null, "accuracy": null, "for_optimality": false}, {"global index": "SuperGLUE-ClozeTest_10724", "prompt": "Read the following passage and answer the question by choosing the best option. Provide only the text of the correct option as your answer.\n\nPassage: In among all the pomp and ceremony surrounding the Apple Music launch, the firm has quietly killed off its Home Sharing feature, outraging many users. Before the iOS 8.4 update, Home Sharing let people share their iTunes music with other iOS devices on the same Wi-Fi network and was a favourite among families. But since Apple rolled out the software the feature has seemingly vanished and there has been no word as to why, leading to angry tweets and forum posts. The iOS 8.4 update 